WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.147 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A lunchbox feels heavy, while a bottle looks full.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:05.719 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Are we measuring the same attribute?

00:00:06.369 --> 00:00:08.588 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine packing for a picnic.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:12.664 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need a balance for mass and a marked container for
capacity.

00:00:12.884 --> 00:00:15.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us choose useful units before measuring.

00:00:16.377 --> 00:00:21.658 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to measure mass and capacity using
scales and combined units.

00:00:22.308 --> 00:00:24.065 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:24.285 --> 00:00:26.869 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can distinguish mass from capacity.

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:29.586 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can read equal scale intervals.

00:00:29.806 --> 00:00:33.911 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can combine kilograms and grams or litres and millilitres.

00:00:34.561 --> 00:00:40.223 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mass describes how heavy an object is; capacity describes
how much a container can hold.

00:00:40.443 --> 00:00:45.331 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use grams or kilograms for mass, and millilitres or litres
for capacity.

00:00:45.551 --> 00:00:48.312 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One kilogram is one thousand grams.

00:00:48.532 --> 00:00:51.262 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One litre is one thousand millilitres.

00:00:51.482 --> 00:00:56.845 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On every scale, count equal intervals before deciding what
one interval represents.

00:00:57.495 --> 00:01:01.658 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A bag has a mass of one kilogram and three hundred and fifty
grams.

00:01:01.878 --> 00:01:07.267 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture one thousand grams as a whole kilogram, with the
smaller amount added alongside.

00:01:07.487 --> 00:01:10.877 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What single gram reading would describe the same mass?

00:01:11.527 --> 00:01:16.877 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One thousand plus three hundred and fifty is one thousand
three hundred and fifty grams.

00:01:17.097 --> 00:01:20.689 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The bag has not become heavier because we changed its name.

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:24.603 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Kilograms and grams are two units for the same attribute.

00:01:25.253 --> 00:01:29.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A jug holds two litres and two hundred and fifty
millilitres.

00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:31.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Convert the whole litres first.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:36.080 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two groups of one thousand millilitres make two thousand
millilitres.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.715 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then include the extra marked amount.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:43.273 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The capacity is two thousand two hundred and fifty
millilitres.

00:01:43.493 --> 00:01:50.318 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On a scale from zero to one thousand with four equal
intervals, each interval would be two hundred and fifty
millilitres.

00:01:50.538 --> 00:01:53.141 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count intervals, not just tick marks.

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:57.429 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Grams and millilitres are not interchangeable labels.

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:01.610 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A balance measures mass; a marked jug measures capacity.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.974 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equal numerical readings do not make those attributes the
same.

00:02:06.194 --> 00:02:09.437 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read the instrument and include the correct unit.

00:02:10.087 --> 00:02:14.458 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A balance points halfway between five hundred and six
hundred grams.

00:02:14.678 --> 00:02:16.520 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What mass does it show?

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.459 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Halfway is fifty grams beyond five hundred, giving five
hundred and fifty grams.

00:02:25.679 --> 00:02:30.274 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The scale is measuring mass, so the answer needs grams, not
millilitres.

00:02:30.924 --> 00:02:34.386 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose the attribute, unit and instrument together.

00:02:34.606 --> 00:02:39.064 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a practical challenge, use a kitchen balance or marked
jug with an adult.

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:44.844 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict a reading, measure carefully, and explain the
difference between your estimate and observation.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:47.894 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.

00:02:48.114 --> 00:02:52.554 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the MathMagic practice, and explain your thinking to
someone.
